From 6bed14e246f23882124cb6efb5f2a6538515d4f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmitry Bulashev <d.bulashev@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 09:58:08 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] pathprobe: name the real dominator of a REJECTED path

The core hook was called after the add_path() loop with
parent_rel->cheapest_total_path as a proxy for the dominating path.
set_cheapest() has not run while a rel is being populated, so the proxy
was usually NULL, and where it was set it was the cheapest total path,
not necessarily the path that won the comparison (a path can lose on
startup cost, pathkeys or parameterization to another one).  A
consumer could not draw a "rejected by" edge.

Core patch: keep old_path when the loop breaks on !accept_new and pass
it to the rejected hook; it stays in the pathlist, so the pointer is
valid during the hook.  The loop can also remove old paths that
new_path dominates before it meets one that dominates new_path (fuzzy
dominance is not transitive).  Those were freed after the hooks, but
got no DISPLACED event and their ids were not forgotten, so a later
path at a reused address could inherit one.  The rejected hook now also
gets that list, which add_path() collects whenever either hook is set.
The signature changes; HAVE_PATHPROBE_REAL_DOMINATOR and
HAVE_PATHPROBE_REJECTED_DOMINATED_LIST in pathnode.h let the extension
build against both versions.

Extension: a REJECTED event records the dominator's path_id; JSON emits
dominator_path_id and other_pathtype, gated by the new coverage key
add_path_rejected_dominator, so the proxy of an older core is never
reported as a dominator.  There is no dominator_seq: the dominator was
accepted earlier, possibly without a recorded event.  Paths a rejected
path removed get DISPLACED events after its REJECTED event, with the
rejected path as displacer (under pathprobe.log_accepted, as in the
accepted hook), and their ids are forgotten.  gap_cost of REJECTED is
now measured against the dominator, so it is non-zero, the text output
shows lost_by for rejected paths, and pathprobe.min_gap filters them.

pathprobe_graph checks that every REJECTED event names a path
accepted into the same rel earlier, never itself, whose type matches
other_pathtype; the Perl model checks the same references and accepts
a REJECTED event at displacer_seq.  The removed-before-rejected case is
not triggered by a test.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
